6bee85f0a405"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-28","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":28,"english":"Accompanied by Vibhīşaņa, that powerful son of the king-Lakşmaņa-after his brother had offered up prayers in his behalf, set off with all speed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072028","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"011c8731a0e033ca4fe099994e9b20fb7a17a81c4d4c9e0f0f1c863b6d53aaad","compact":"c90593a777ac733d69cb7576542d7d1930ec1dd263c9bd78c424b26715fac023","nasal":"c90593a777ac733d69cb7576542d7d1930ec1dd263c9bd78c424b26715fac023"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-29","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":29,"english":"Surrounded by thousands of monkeys, Hanumān as well as Vibhīşaņa together with his counsellors speedily went in the wake of Lakş mana.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.422,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072029","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d4d5be6a880ea82fb1ee10d92d3c9114122a4a7807fe0d89135d3a3d1a23dcc9","compact":"591d5e0360288ad2875d89a513fc0fd1bb3642347c219a387872c92bfddbc848","nasal":"591d5e0360288ad2875d89a513fc0fd1bb3642347c219a387872c92bfddbc848"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-30","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":30,"english":"And strongly encompassed by that mighty monkey-host, (Laksmana) saw the forces of the bear-king stationed on the way.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072030","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"684fde4d63379c0b28b4dd1ae513ec842cc5228eab7a6d03e2e8a64c650d8e80","compact":"1581edcb556eaf5a332fe38652aaf411cd0f300e1495b8e345d2a0447e52e82e","nasal":"1581edcb556eaf5a332fe38652aaf411cd0f300e1495b8e345d2a0447e52e82e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-31","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":31,"english":"Having proceeded far, Sumitra's Sumitra's son-enhancer of the delight of friends, from a distance discovered the forces of the Rākşasmonarch, drawn up in battle-array.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.771,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072031","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6b1ffd33f3bdd7714e23dc66aa14d0ca06e4cd158c7aa9ef9ed6a04c892e9171","compact":"8d73a5dd90a1e099db0cc8d0d91b08daed9eeeb0cb0b0903e8e8816b19474df4","nasal":"66ae27a64382c8c0920c286e2a89e2aa942ec34057d87aee746ccfa8df5b4915"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-32","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":32,"english":"That subduer of enemies-Raghu's sonbearing his bow in his hand, coming upon him whose martial might lay in illusion, stopped (there) for conquering his foe agreeably to the ordinance * of Brahma. *The ordinance was that Indrajit should be slain while remaining outside Nikumbhilā, his rites not yet finished.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.754,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-30"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072032","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c6ac01a9017d6ced9ab099d8322504e70ba87dae0fcbea5197c12397d20f5caa","compact":"f4a3c9f428e6c9588ff16350a326591a4bee2bcba96845ae4f1fc0618ff6ad09","nasal":"ba2174be2f48d73abde3bf970ded9a5c15173964bf7d57d1d8e3bb83e79576a8"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-72-33","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":72,"verse":33,"english":"Accompanied by Vibhīşaņa, the heroic Angada, and the Wind-god's offspring, the powerful son of the king dived into the forces of the foe, various, blazing with burnished arms, dense with standards, and thronging with mighty cars,* capable of striking terror (into the hearts of the spectators), instinct with immeasurable energy, and resembling darkness. *Maharathai (ab.) may also mean with mighty carwarriors.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-85-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6072033","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"491b52e2d46b92f7790735c8d15a913d639b6c62817a1ca2ee7e45e9b10c20d5","compact":"100244c167c251e83a4bb88cad26ceb7e5511fea41d8ba6d291b8a7ae3119f78","nasal":"100244c167c251e83a4bb88cad26ceb7e5511fea41d8ba6d291b8a7ae3119f78"}}]
